```javascript
function reverseAdjectives(sentence) {
  // Split the sentence into an array of words
  const words = sentence.split(" ");

  // Reverse the order of adjectives in the array
  const adjectives = words.filter(word => word.match(/^[a-zA-Z]+$/)); // Filter out non-alphabetical words (e.g., articles, prepositions)
  const reversedAdjectives = adjectives.reverse();

  // Reconstruct the sentence with the reversed adjectives
  const reversedSentence = reversedAdjectives.join(" ") + " " + words.filter(word => !word.match(/^[a-zA-Z]+$/)).join(" ");

  return reversedSentence;
}

// Test the function with the given example sentence
const originalSentence = "Beautiful green trees are in the park";
const reversedSentence = reverseAdjectives(originalSentence);
console.log(reversedSentence); // Output: "Green beautiful trees are in the park"
```

This function first splits the input sentence into an array of words using the `split()` method. It then filters out any non-alphabetical words (e.g., articles, prepositions) using a regular expression, and reverses the order of the remaining adjectives using the `reverse()` method. Finally, it reconstructs the sentence by joining the reversed adjectives back together with a space, followed by the non-adjectival words.

Note that this function assumes that adjectives are words that contain only letters (i.e., they do not contain numbers or special characters). If you want to handle adjectives that contain numbers or special characters, you may need to modify the regular expression used to filter out non-alphabetical words.

