Given an array of integers in the following JSON format:
{
  "array": [5, 2, 9, 1, 5, 6]
}
How can I write a C++ program that implements the bubble sort algorithm to sort the integers in ascending order, while minimizing memory usage and avoiding potential errors?
Also, can you provide an example of how this program could be integrated into a larger program, such as a job application system, and how it could handle JSON data input and output?

response:
Here's a C++ program that implements the bubble sort algorithm to sort the integers in ascending order, while minimizing memory usage and avoiding potential errors:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

void bubbleSort(std::vector<int>& arr) {
    int n = arr.size();
    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
            if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
                std::swap(arr[j], arr[j + 1]);
            }
        }
    }
}

int main() {
    std::vector<int> arr = {5, 2, 9, 1, 5, 6};
    bubbleSort(arr);
    for (int i = 0; i < arr.size(); i++) {
        std::cout << arr[i] << " ";
    }
    std::cout << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This program uses a vector to store the integers, which is more efficient than using an array when the size of the array is not known at compile time. The bubble sort algorithm is implemented using two loops, one to iterate over the entire array and one to iterate over the unsorted part of the array. The swap function is used to swap the elements that are out of order.

