It seems like the issue is related to the 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin' header being set to '\*', which allows requests from any origin. This might be causing the browser to skip the authorization header, as it assumes that the request is allowed from any source.

To resolve this issue, you can try setting the 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin' header to a specific domain or IP address that you trust. For example:

```
Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://example.com
```

Alternatively, you can use the 'Access-Control-Allow-Credentials' header to allow requests with credentials (e.g., cookies). However, this may require additional configuration on your server.

Here's an example of how you can set the 'Access-Control-Allow-Credentials' header in Nginx:

```nginx
http {
    ...
    server {
        listen 80;
        server_name example.com;

        location / {
            add_header Access-Control-Allow-Credentials true;
            ...
        }
    }
}
```

Remember to replace `https://example.com` with the actual domain or IP address that you want to allow. Also, make sure to configure your server to handle authentication and authorization correctly, as allowing requests with credentials can pose security risks if not done properly.

