Here's a complete example:
```java
public class Product {
    private String name;
    private double price;

    public Product(String name, double price) {
        this.name = name;
        this.price = price;
    }

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public double getPrice() {
        return price;
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Product[] products = new Product[5];

        products[0] = new Product("Headphones", 50);
        products[1] = new Product("Headphones", 50);
        products[2] = new Product("Headphones", 50);
        products[3] = new Product("Microphone", 100);
        products[4] = new Product("Microphone", 100);
        products[5] = new Product("Speaker", 150);
        products[6] = new Product("Speaker", 150);
        products[7] = new Product("Speaker", 150);

        double totalCost = 0;
        for (Product product : products) {
            totalCost += product.getPrice();
        }

        System.out.println("Total cost: " + totalCost);
    }
}
```
This code will output:
```
Total cost: 850.0
```
This is a simple example, but you can modify it to fit your specific needs. For example, you can add more products, calculate the total cost based on quantity, or display the results in a different format.

