Here's a simple React utility that removes widows from text:

```javascript
import React, { useState } from 'react';

const RemoveWidows = ({ text }) => {
  const [widowedText, setWidowedText] = useState(text);

  const handleRemoveWidows = () => {
    const words = widowedText.split(' ');
    const newWidowedText = [];

    for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
      if (i % 2 === 0) {
        newWidowedText.push(words[i]);
      }
    }

    setWidowedText(newWidowedText.join(' '));
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={handleRemoveWidows}>Remove Widows</button>
      <p>{widowedText}</p>
    </div>
  );
};

export default RemoveWidows;
```

To use this component, simply pass in the text you want to remove widows from as a prop:

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import RemoveWidows from './RemoveWidows';

const App = () => {
  return (
    <div>
      <RemoveWidows text="Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit." />
    </div>
  );
};

export default App;
```

This component will display the input text and a button to remove widows. When the button is clicked, the text will be processed to remove every other word, effectively removing widows.

